{
    "title": "Presidential Determination With Respect to the Efforts of Foreign Governments Regarding Trafficking in Persons",
    "type": "Presidential Document",
    "date": "December 19, 2025",
    "url": "https://www.govinfo.gov/content/pkg/FR-2025-12-19/pdf/2025-23569.pdf",
    "summary": "The White House has decided to limit certain types of U.S. government assistance to a number of countries because of their poor efforts to combat human trafficking, while allowing targeted exceptions where it says doing so serves anti-trafficking goals or U.S. interests.\n\nIn practical terms, the United States will withhold nonhumanitarian, nontrade-related aid from Afghanistan, Chad, Iran, South Sudan, and Venezuela in the 2026 fiscal year unless they improve. It will also withhold that aid and block funding for officials\u2019 participation in U.S. educational and cultural exchange programs for Belarus, Burma, China, Cuba, North Korea, Eritrea, Macau, Nicaragua, and Russia. The U.S. representatives at the International Monetary Fund and multilateral development banks are instructed to vote against loans to many of these governments, except for humanitarian relief, trade-related support, or basic-needs projects not run by the government.\n\nThere are notable exceptions. The U.S. will allow all forms of nonhumanitarian, nontrade-related aid and exchanges to continue for Cambodia, Laos, Papua New Guinea, Sudan, and Syria. Afghanistan and Chad may still receive support from international financial institutions. Belarus, Eritrea, Macau, and Russia can continue to participate in educational and cultural exchanges. South Sudan can receive global health assistance. Venezuela can receive most types of civilian assistance, but not military or peacekeeping-related support. For Sint Maarten, the U.S. will restrict most aid and seek to block international loans, but will allow participation in educational and cultural exchanges.\n\nWho stands to benefit? Efforts to prevent human trafficking and assist victims may gain leverage, as these measures are intended to press governments to meet minimum standards. Humanitarian programs, health initiatives, and basic-needs development\u2014especially those run outside the targeted governments\u2014can continue, which supports vulnerable communities and nongovernmental partners. Countries granted partial waivers retain avenues for people-to-people ties and essential services.\n\nWho may be negatively affected? Governments facing restrictions could see reduced access to development finance and certain forms of U.S. assistance. Officials and students in some countries will miss exchange opportunities, which may limit dialogue and skills building. There is also a risk that ordinary citizens feel the impact if government-run development projects slow, though humanitarian and basic-needs aid are protected.\n\nOverall, this step signals ongoing U.S. pressure on governments to address trafficking, with carve-outs designed to shield humanitarian efforts and maintain select channels of engagement.",
